/**
\page AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo Analyzing a Crash Dump

\tableofcontents

A MITK minidump (a <tt>.dmp</tt> file) records the stacks of all threads, the
CPU context, and the list of loaded modules - not source code and not symbols.
Turning it into function names, files, and line numbers needs two things:

-# the debug symbols built from the <em>exact same sources</em> as the binary
   that crashed, and
-# a tool that walks the dump against those symbols.

This page is the operational walkthrough. For the facility itself - what a dump
contains, where dumps live, retention, and the privacy implications - see
\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Pieces Getting the pieces

- <b>The dump.</b> From the crash-dump database
  (\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Storage), or reproduce one deliberately
  (\ref AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Reproduce).
- <b>Matching symbols.</b> The <tt>-symbols.zip</tt> produced by the
  \c package-symbols target for that release
  (\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols_Archive). A dump only decodes against
  symbols from the identical build; a different build, even of the same
  sources, will not match.
- <b>A tool.</b> Platform specific; see the per-OS sections below and
  \ref AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Tools.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Windows Windows

Windows debug info lives in PDBs (shipped inside the symbols archive, not with
the application). Use \c cdb or WinDbg from the Windows SDK "Debugging Tools for
Windows". A PDB is matched to its DLL by an embedded signature, so no manual
pairing is needed - just make the extracted symbols directory a symbol path.

\code
cdb -z crash.dmp -y <extracted-symbols-dir> -c "!analyze -v; k; q"
\endcode

Interactively in WinDbg: <em>File > Open Crash Dump</em>, then

\code
.sympath+ <extracted-symbols-dir>
!analyze -v      REM triage: faulting frame and probable cause
.ecxr            REM switch to the context of the crash
k                REM call stack of the crashing thread
\endcode

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Linux Linux

Linux binaries are shipped unstripped, and the bulk of the debug info sits
beside them as split DWARF (see \ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols); both are in
the symbols archive. Minidumps are not a native ELF format, so a debugger does
not open them directly - convert the debug info to a Breakpad symbol file and
walk the dump against it.

The general flow (exact invocation depends on the tool you pick):

\code
# 1. Produce a Breakpad .sym from the binary (resolving its split DWARF).
dump_syms libMitkCore.so > MitkCore.sym

# 2. Its first line is: MODULE Linux x86_64 <BUILD-ID> MitkCore
#    Place the file where the walker expects it:
mkdir -p symbols/MitkCore/<BUILD-ID>
mv MitkCore.sym symbols/MitkCore/<BUILD-ID>/MitkCore.sym

# 3. Walk the dump against the symbol tree.
minidump-stackwalk crash.dmp symbols/
\endcode

Repeat step 1 for each module whose frames you need resolved.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_macOS macOS

macOS debug info lives in <tt>.dSYM</tt> bundles (see
\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols), carried in the symbols archive. The flow is
the same as on Linux, reading from the <tt>.dSYM</tt> instead of split DWARF:

\code
dump_syms MitkCore.framework.dSYM > MitkCore.sym
# then the same symbol-tree layout and walker as on Linux
\endcode

Two macOS specifics:

- Dumps are written to
  <tt>&lt;user data location&gt;/&lt;organization&gt;/&lt;application&gt;/CrashDumps</tt>,
  as on the other platforms.
- Only hard-crash dumps are produced on macOS. On-demand snapshots and the
  UI-freeze watchdog are unavailable there, because Crashpad's macOS client has
  no <tt>DumpWithoutCrash</tt> equivalent (macOS drives dumps through a Mach
  exception server, on an actual crash). Hard-crash capture is unaffected.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Tools Tools

MITK does not ship the cross-platform symbol tools; obtain them separately. If
you have no existing preference, the following are options, listed without
endorsement:

- \c dump_syms to produce Breakpad <tt>.sym</tt> files - implementations exist
  from Mozilla (Rust) and from Google Breakpad.
- A minidump walker such as <tt>minidump-stackwalk</tt> from the rust-minidump
  project, or Breakpad's <tt>minidump_stackwalk</tt>.
- On Windows, \c cdb / WinDbg (Windows SDK) read the PDBs directly and need no
  symbol conversion.

Follow the chosen tool's own documentation for details such as how it resolves
split DWARF or dSYM input.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Reading Reading the result

- <b>Crashing thread.</b> On Windows \c !analyze -v names it and the faulting
  frame; with a walker, it is the thread carrying the faulting instruction.
- <b>Resolution differs by origin.</b> MITK's own frames resolve to file and
  line; frames in prebuilt third-party dependencies (ITK, VTK, Qt, ...) resolve
  to function name plus offset only. Those dependencies are built separately and
  never carry MITK's symbol flags (see \ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Symbols), so
  this is expected, not a setup error.
- <b>All frames blank, or a signature/build-id warning.</b> The symbols do not
  match the binary: usually the wrong <tt>-symbols.zip</tt> for this exact
  build, or a stripped binary with no symbols archived.

\section AnalyzingCrashDumpsHowTo_Reproduce Reproducing a dump

To practise the workflow, or to verify symbols resolve before a real incident,
produce a dump on demand with the \c org.mitk.gui.qt.crashtest plugin (a QA aid,
off by default; build with \c MITK_BUILD_org.mitk.gui.qt.crashtest, see
\ref CrashDumpFacilityPage_Testing). Its Crash Test view offers one button per
failure path: a hard crash, an unhandled mitk::Exception, and a UI freeze of
configurable duration. Start the application with <tt>--ui-watchdog 5</tt> to
let the freeze watchdog react within seconds instead of the default timeout.
*/
